---
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import { CORE_COMMIT_SHORT, CORE_RELEASE_TAG, CORE_REPO, PYPI_URL, roadmapSnapshot, specs, versions } from '../data/facts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const snapshotUrl = `${CORE_REPO}/commit/${roadmapSnapshot.commit}`;
const snapshotShort = roadmapSnapshot.commit.slice(0, 7);
---

<Base
  title="Vision & Roadmap"
  description="RoboLang's research direction, its development status on 4 November 2026, the horizons it is working through, the Gate 2 interoperability sequence and the planned work beyond it. Nothing on this page runs on a robot."
>
  <PageHead slot="head" index="05" label="Vision & Roadmap" title="Vision & roadmap">
    Where RoboLang is trying to go, and how that differs from what you can install today.
  </PageHead>

  <div class="prose">
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>Plans and features are marked apart.</strong> Stages marked Released are in the
        package on PyPI; stages marked Delivered are in the core repository but not yet released.
        Nothing on this page runs on a robot, enforces a safety limit or includes an AI model, and
        plans may change.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Vision</h2>
    <p>
      The research question behind RoboLang is whether one small, precisely specified task language
      can sit between the people and AI systems that decide what a robot should do and the varied
      software that makes robots move.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Interoperable tasks.</strong> The same task description, checked once, understood by
        different robots through adapters that declare what each robot can do.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Multi-robot communication.</strong> Structured, versioned messages for handing work
        from one robot to another, in place of free text.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI-generated programs.</strong> A language strict enough that a task written by a
        language model can be rejected with a precise reason before it reaches anything physical.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The project builds toward this in order: a correct language first, then execution in simulation,
      then interoperability. Each stage is meant to be verifiable before the next begins.
    </p>

    <h2 id="status">Development status, {roadmapSnapshot.date}</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>A dated snapshot.</strong> The statuses below were read from the core repository at
        commit <a href={snapshotUrl}><code>{snapshotShort}</code></a> on {roadmapSnapshot.date}. The
        command examples and technical facts elsewhere on this site stay pinned to release
        <code>{CORE_RELEASE_TAG}</code> (core commit <code>{CORE_COMMIT_SHORT}</code>), which is what
        <code>pip install robolang</code> installs and what they were captured and verified against.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Released</strong> means in distribution {versions.distribution} on
        <a href={PYPI_URL}>PyPI</a>. <strong>Delivered</strong> means merged into the core repository
        after that release. <strong>Approved</strong> means a decision is recorded; it does not
        authorize implementation. <strong>Planned</strong> means the work has not started.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>In release {versions.distribution}</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Stage</th>
            <th scope="col">Focus</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Language 1.1 and 1.2</th>
            <td>
              Specification and grammar, parser, static validation, versioned IR, and conditions
              combined with <code>and</code>, <code>or</code> and <code>not</code>.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Released</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Foundation 1 (RL-BASIC-01)</th>
            <td>
              Simulation-only execution: explicit executor states, preconditions evaluated against a
              declared world file, fail-closed behavior and a deterministic run record
              (<code>robolang simulate</code>).
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Released</strong>, simulation only. Signed off 2026-10-27
              (<a href={specs.foundation1Evidence}>evidence</a>)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Gate 1 (RL-ARCH-02)</th>
            <td>
              Core {versions.core} message and capability contracts, and an in-process reference
              simulation with an AI planner stub and two robot stubs (<code>robolang arch02</code>).
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Released</strong>, simulation only. Signed off 2026-10-27
              (<a href={specs.gate1Evidence}>evidence</a>)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Language 1.3 (goals)</th>
            <td>
              A required <code>goal</code> that decides success, outcome conditions and typed
              <code>sensor</code> parameters.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Released</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Goal bridge to Core</th>
            <td>
              Core-shaped outcomes in every 1.3 run record, <code>export-goal</code>, and
              <code>delegate</code> with a peer robot, commitment, lease and custody.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Released</strong>, simulation only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">CORE-1</th>
            <td>Task IR reader with re-validation: <code>--ir</code> on every command.</td>
            <td><strong>Released</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Language 1.4 (branching)</th>
            <td>
              Bounded <code>if</code> / <code>else</code> and <code>fail</code>, and
              <code>validate --world</code>, which checks every path against a world before a run.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Released.</strong> Accepted on the authoring benchmark 2026-10-29: branching
              goal correctness 100% with the full reference and 95% with the grammar only
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">First PyPI release</th>
            <td>Distribution {versions.distribution}: <code>pip install robolang</code>.</td>
            <td><strong>Released</strong> 2026-10-30</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>In the core repository since the release</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Stage</th>
            <th scope="col">Focus</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={roadmapSnapshot.core02Freeze}>Core 0.2</a></th>
            <td>
              Alternatives, <code>met_alternative</code> and <code>declared</code> facts. Core 0.1
              stays the default; <a href={roadmapSnapshot.core02Flag}><code>--core 0.2</code></a> opts
              in on <code>simulate</code>, <code>export-goal</code> and <code>delegate</code>.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Frozen</strong> 2026-10-30. Not yet released</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={roadmapSnapshot.h3Acceptance}>ROS 2 backend in physics simulation</a></th>
            <td>
              The same <code>.rob</code> tasks and goals run through ROS 2 and MoveIt in Gazebo, with
              goals verified from camera, TF and gripper observations instead of simulator ground
              truth. A separate, optional package.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Delivered</strong>, physics simulation only. Acceptance 2026-10-31: 33/33 runs
              succeeded, 51/51 goal checks agreed with an independent oracle, no false success
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={roadmapSnapshot.lang15}>Language 1.5</a></th>
            <td>
              Step failure handlers, <code>&lt;step&gt; else &#123; … &#125;</code>, with Task IR 1.4 and
              run record 1.4.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Delivered</strong> 2026-11-04 in the front end and simulation. Teaching it in the
              language reference and the benchmark is <a href={roadmapSnapshot.teaching15}>proposed</a>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Physical robots</th>
            <td>Running tasks on real hardware, through adapters and local safety controls.</td>
            <td>Not started and not authorized</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="horizons">Horizons</h2>
    <p>
      The project works through four horizons. A horizon ends when its exit criteria hold, not on a
      date. The strategy that defines them is itself still a proposal awaiting owner approval.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Horizon</th>
            <th scope="col">Exit criteria</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">H1 Trust the basics</th>
            <td>
              <code>pip install robolang</code> works; the language reference ships and is tested; AI
              models make the right write-or-decline decision on at least 97% of benchmark tasks.
            </td>
            <td>Install and reference met. Decision accuracy for every model still being measured</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">H2 Express real requests</th>
            <td>
              Branching goal correctness at least 90%; false programs on inexpressible tasks at most
              5%, even with the grammar only; Core 0.2 frozen.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Met</strong> 2026-10-30</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">H3 Leave the symbolic world</th>
            <td>
              One benchmark task family passes end to end in physics simulation, verified through
              sensor observations; an independent Core 0.2 implementation interoperates (Gate 2).
            </td>
            <td>First criterion <strong>met</strong> 2026-11-01. Gate 2 open</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">H4 Earn hardware</th>
            <td>
              A written safety case and owner authorization for physical mode. Never before H3 is
              met.
            </td>
            <td>Not started</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="gate-2">Gate 2 development sequence</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>Gate 2 has not passed.</strong> No independent implementation exists yet. Gate 2 tests
        interoperability in simulation, through a test binding between processes. It does not cover a
        real network transport, production authentication (signatures stay simulation-only), hardware
        safety or physical execution.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Step</th>
            <th scope="col">Work</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Specification</th>
            <td>
              The <a href={specs.gate2}>Gate 2 specification</a>: a participant built in a different
              codebase, from the published documents alone, works with the reference implementation.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Approved</strong> 2026-10-27</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">INTEROP-1 and 2</th>
            <td>
              External-process reference self-test, decision review, traceability audit and a first
              frozen bundle on Core 0.1.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Completed</strong> by 2026-10-28. The Core 0.1 bundle was never delivered</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={roadmapSnapshot.gate2Freeze}>Core 0.2 bundle</a></th>
            <td>
              The frozen specification bundle on Core 0.2: specifications, examples, conformance
              fixtures and a SHA-256 manifest.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Frozen</strong> 2026-11-02</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={roadmapSnapshot.interop3Protocol}>INTEROP-3</a></th>
            <td>
              An independent implementation, written in a separate codebase from the frozen bundle
              alone, behind an auditable clean-room information barrier.
            </td>
            <td>Protocol <strong>approved</strong> 2026-11-02. Launch not yet authorized</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">INTEROP-4</th>
            <td>
              Verification: conformance tests, interoperability scenarios, deterministic repetition,
              evidence and owner sign-off.
            </td>
            <td>Planned</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="beyond">Beyond</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Workstream</th>
            <th scope="col">Planned capabilities</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Language</th>
            <td>Bounded repetition, typed values and comparisons, local bindings and structured data</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Foundation 3</th>
            <td>Concurrency, portable skills and heterogeneous robot support</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">AI-native v2.0</th>
            <td>Provider-independent AI task generation, validation and benchmarking</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>These are planning guidance.</strong> They do not authorize implementation and are not
      release commitments. Each workstream starts only with its own authorization and, where it
      changes the language, the IR or Core, its own versioned specification. A new language feature
      also needs benchmark tasks that fail without it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Language versions (1.1 to 1.5) name the syntax of <code>.rob</code> files and are separate from
      the milestones above, and both are separate from the distribution version of the Python package;
      see <a href="/get-started/#versions">the version numbers</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full plan, with the scope and exit criteria for each stage, is
      the <a href={specs.developmentPlan}>development plan</a> in the core repository, linked as it was at
      release <code>{CORE_RELEASE_TAG}</code>; the status snapshot above is newer. The architecture
      the stages build toward is <a href={specs.rfc001}>RFC-001</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Physical robots</h2>
    <p>
      Running RoboLang against real hardware is outside the current scope of the project. The supported
      package cannot do it: its simulations are symbolic and run in one process. The ROS 2 backend
      runs only in a physics simulator and is not authorized for hardware; physical mode needs a
      written safety case and an explicit owner decision first. The older prototype code in the
      repository has never been validated against ROS 2 or a robot. Please do not connect RoboLang to
      physical equipment.
    </p>

    <h2>Help shape it</h2>
    <p>
      The most useful input at this stage is on the language and the contracts: what is awkward to
      express, which diagnostics are unclear, what a consumer of the JSON IR or a run record needs, and
      where Core {versions.core} is ambiguous. See <a href="/contribute/">Contribute</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
